# Where chorus-sub-v1.json came from: the subwoofer's bass-management crossover
# (docs/hardware/subwoofer.md, version 1), one LR4 at 80 Hz for 48 kHz. Its low branch is the
# subwoofer's low-pass and its high branch the high-pass a main plays in a bonded set with a
# subwoofer (docs/dsp.md, chain item 8), at the chain's default crossover_hz. The record is
# committed as it was exported, byte for byte: crates/dsp/tests/design_record.rs holds the file
# to the sha256 below (and to the same digest written in the test), so an edit by hand fails the
# test. To refresh it: fixtures/README.md, "design-record/".
#
# The command is the call the acoustics package's README documents for writing a record, run with
# the package installed from the release tag (uv, rootless). The package and its module are
# written as placeholders: the library is the owner's and is not named here. Release v1.50.0 was
# the current release on the day of the export; between v1.49.0 (the compact's and the two-way's
# release) and v1.50.0 the acoustics package changed only its own version number.

record = chorus-sub-v1.json
source = the acoustics package of the owner's shared Python library
release = v1.50.0
command = uv run --package <the acoustics package> python -c "from <its module> import *; write_record(design_record('chorus-sub-v1', [linkwitz_riley(48000, 80)], response_hz=(20, 40, 80, 160, 320)), 'chorus-sub-v1.json')"
exported = 2026-10-06
schema = speaker-design-record
version = 1
sha256 = 7efc717e20999a509a2c5790601803249dd0462a750836cbf6f0363433b6e0ed
